{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And you shall write them on the doorposts.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: מזזות is written (with a defective ו), to teach that a mezuzah is required only on one side — that is, on one side alone, and it is the right side. And this is what they said: 'your house' — according to your manner of entry; that is, on the right of its entrance, for when a person enters, he begins with his right foot first. And our Rabbis of blessed memory have already explained to us which buildings are obligated and which are exempt: as they said, a house that does not have four cubits by four cubits, and a gatehouse, a portico, a balcony, a lavatory, a bathhouse, a tanner's workshop, and a immersion-house — these are exempt from mezuzah, for it says 'your house': just as your house is specially designated for dwelling and for dignity, these excluded ones are not specially designated for dwelling and for dignity. And they said further: the Temple Mount, the chambers, and the courtyards are exempt from mezuzah, for it says 'your house' — the house that is yours, that is, a profane house; these excluded ones are sacred and are not 'your house.' The same applies to a synagogue, which is holy: there is no obligation of mezuzah unless it contains a dwelling for the synagogue attendant. And they said: all the chambers in the Temple had no mezuzah except the Chamber of Parhedrin, in which there was a dwelling for the High Priest for seven days in the year." "<b>And you need</b> to know that from within this commandment the principles of the Torah concerning faith are made clear to us — the creation of the world, divine providence, and prophecy — for in the first paragraph the matter of the unity and the study of Torah are mentioned, and in the second paragraph the truth of reward and punishment, and the remembrance of the exodus from Egypt, through which the signs and wonders and famous miracles were performed, obligates these principles and testifies to three things that are the foundation and root of faith, and they are: creation, providence, and prophecy. And our Rabbis of blessed memory, the sages of truth, received that the place for affixing the mezuzah is at the end of the upper third, and so it was explained in the Yerushalmi. The reason for this is that it alludes to the end of the structure, which is the third honor, called by the name שי״ן דל״ת and אל״ף דל״ת. And for this reason we write the name שי״ן דל״ת on the back of the parchment of the mezuzah, so that it be visible opposite the opening through the hole. And for this reason it is called by the name מזוזה, which has the numerical value of the name אל״ף דל״ת, and it too is called 'the Guardian of Israel'; and so the mezuzah guards the person from without while He is within. And according to its plain sense, the reason we write the name Shaddai on the mezuzah is that the nations think that the success of houses depends on the arrangement of the stars; and because this name overpowers and conquers the arrangement, therefore we write it to allude that from Him the influx and the good enter the houses, not from the stellar arrangement. And so Eliphaz said (Job 22): ״ומה יפעל שדי למו״, and adjacent to it: ״והוא מלא בתיהם טוב״. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said in the Yerushalmi, tractate Pe'ah: Artaban sent to Rabbi a pearl. He said to him: Send me something worth more than it or equal to it. He sent him a mezuzah. He said to him: I am sending you a pearl that has no measure, and you are sending me something worth one polir?! He sent word to him: Mine is superior to yours, for yours is a thing that has a limit to its value, while mine has no limit to its value; and not only that — yours requires guarding, while mine guards you, as it is written (Mishlei 6:22): ״בשכבך תשמור עליך והקיצות היא תשיחך״ — thus far. And one who affixes a mezuzah must recite the blessing 'who has sanctified us with His commandments and commanded us to affix a mezuzah,' and must also recite shehechiyanu, according to the rule for a commandment that comes from time to time." "<b>And in your gates.</b> To include the gates of courtyards, the gates of provinces, and the gates of towns, for thus our Rabbis of blessed memory said: whether gates of provinces or gates of towns, there is in them a commandment to affix a mezuzah, because it says וכתבתם על מזזות ביתך ובשעריך. But only when there is in them a dwelling for the gate of the city — for from what they interpreted, 'your house' means a house designated for dwelling, we learn from this that if it is not designated for dwelling there is no obligation at all. And this is the sense of 'your house and your gates' — that is, 'your gates' is analogous to 'your house.'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:46:16.642356740Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}